Column 1 has a Red card, Column 2 has Yellow, Column 3 has Green, and the Slot has Purple. No valid moves can be made because there are no matching colors in the columns for the slot. Clicking any of the columns or the slot would be ineffective. The game is lost!\n\n\nThe board state with distinct colors in each column and the slot creates an immediate \"game over\" condition. This occurs because the moved card's color in the holding slot prevents any subsequent valid moves from any column or the slot itself.  This is possible because the number of columns is less than the number of colors. The key setup relies on matching an initial single-card column with the slot card, then triggering the color mismatch. This results in the complete blocking of the board.\n\n\nThe losing condition arises when the slot card has no matching colors in the columns, locking the game. However, a single card push can also trigger an immediate win if a matching card is revealed in the columns beneath. The requirement of explicitly designing the game with possible losing states needs careful configurations. The initial losing move must also prevent all subsequent moves, including moving the card from the slot. The user prompt emphasizes losing is the goal.\n\n\nGiả sử bàn chơi có $C$ cột (đều còn bài) và 1 Slot tạm đang chứa lá bài màu $M$: Để Slot tạm cũng bị vô hiệu hóa hoàn toàn (không thể bấm vào Slot để trả bài lại cột vừa lấy ra), toàn bộ $C$ cột trên bàn không được có cột nào lộ màu $M$ ở đáy. Để C cột trên bàn cũng không thể ghép với nhau, đáy của mỗi cột phải có màu khác nhau. Nếu số màu (trừ màu M) ít hơn số cột (C), một số cột phải chia sẻ màu chung, đảm bảo có một nước đi. The number of colors on the bottom of the column must equal or exceed the number of the columns, to ensure at least one move is possible.\n\n\nThe losing condition hinges on having the number of colors on the column bottoms exceed the number of columns by at least one. This is essential to guarantee a completely blocked board state after a single \"wrong\" move.  The Pigeonhole Principle dictates that if the number of colors equals the number of columns, a losing state isn't possible, as some columns must share colors, ensuring at least one valid move. For instance, with 3 columns and 3 colors, a loss cannot occur because there would be duplicate colors to enable matching moves. The mathematical proof confirms that a sufficient difference between colors and columns is a MUST.\n\n\nThe analysis shows that with the same number of colors as columns, a losing state with no valid moves is mathematically impossible due to the Pigeonhole Principle.  Having the number of colors exceed the number of columns by at least one is the critical factor for a winnable *and* losable game. With 3 columns and 4 colors, a specific setup can lock the slot card, leading to a losing condition with no further moves. This hinges on ensuring a color mismatch between the slot and the column bottoms.\n\nThe goal is to verify the game's losing states and design a solvable level with traps. A 3-column, 4-color configuration is under evaluation to ensure immediate \"game over\" states via a Node script that verifies valid moves. The objective is to design for solvable states and instant loss conditions with a clear win scenario to validate the level design. A key metric is to ensure the script's outcome correctly identifies 0 valid moves at designated steps.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"node -e \"\nfunction getBottomGroupCount(col) {\n  if (col.length === 0) return 0;\n  const c = col[col.length - 1];\n  let k = 0;\n  for (let i = col.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    if (col[i] === c) k++; else break;\n  }\n  return k;\n}\nfunction findColTarget(columns, color, startIdx) {\n  const n = columns.length;\n  for (let s = 1; s < n; s++) {\n    const idx = (startIdx + s) % n;\n    if (columns[idx].length > 0 && columns[idx][columns[idx].length - 1] === color) return idx;\n  }\n  for (let s = 1; s < n; s++) {\n    const idx = (startIdx + s) % n;\n    if (columns[idx].length === 0) return idx;\n  }\n  return -1;\n}\nfunction findSlotTarget(columns, color) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < columns.length; i++) {\n    if (columns[i].length > 0 && columns[i][columns[i].length - 1] === color) return i;\n  }\n  for (let i = 0; i < columns.length; i++) {\n    if (columns[i].length === 0) return i;\n  }\n  return -1;\n}\nfunction stepState(state, action, targetCount) {\n  const columns = state.columns.map(c => [...c]);\n  let slot = state.slot;\n  if (action.type === 'col') {\n    const idx = action.idx;\n    const col = columns[idx];\n    if (col.length === 0) return null;\n    const k = getBottomGroupCount(col);\n    const color = col[col.length - 1];\n    const t = findColTarget(columns, color, idx);\n    if (t !== -1) {\n      col.splice(col.length - k, k);\n      for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) columns[t].push(color);\n      if (columns[t].length === targetCount && getBottomGroupCount(columns[t]) === targetCount) {\n        columns[t] = [];\n      }\n      return { columns, slot };\n    }\n    if (!slot) {\n      col.pop();\n      slot = color;\n      return { columns, slot };\n    }\n    return null;\n  } else {\n    if (!slot) return null;\n    const t = findSlotTarget(columns, slot);\n    if (t === -1) return null;\n    const color = slot;\n    slot = null;\n    columns[t].push(color);\n    if (columns[t].length === targetCount && getBottomGroupCount(columns[t]) === targetCount) {\n      columns[t] = [];\n    }\n    return { columns, slot };\n  }\n}\nfunction getValidActions(state, targetCount) {\n  const actions = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < state.columns.length; i++) {\n    const a = { type: 'col', idx: i, label: 'C' + (i + 1) };\n    if (stepState(state, a, targetCount)) actions.push(a);\n  }\n  const sa = { type: 'slot', label: 'Slot' };\n  if (stepState(state, sa, targetCount)) actions.push(sa);\n  return actions;\n}\nfunction isWin(state) {\n  return !state.slot && state.columns.every(c => c.length === 0);\n}\nfunction canWin(init, targetCount) {\n  let queue = [{ state: init, path: [] }];\n  const visited = new Set([JSON.stringify(init)]);\n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    const { state, path } = queue.shift();\n    if (isWin(state)) return path;\n    if (path.length >= 14) continue;\n    for (const a of getValidActions(state, targetCount)) {\n      const next = stepState(state, a, targetCount);\n      const k = JSON.stringify(next);\n      if (!visited.has(k)) {\n        visited.add(k);\n        queue.push({ state: next, path: [...path, a.label] });\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nconst cards = ['R','R','R','Y','Y','Y','B','B','B','P','P','P'];\nfunction shuffle(a) {\n  for (let i = a.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {\n    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));\n    [a[i], a[j]] = [a[j], a[i]];\n  }\n  return a;\n}\n\nfor (let it = 0; it < 20000; it++) {\n  const s = shuffle([...cards]);\n  const cols = [s.slice(0,4), s.slice(4,8), s.slice(8,12)];\n  // Require all 3 initial bottom cards to be distinct so Step 1 forces pushing to Slot!\n  const b0 = cols[0][3], b1 = cols[1][3], b2 = cols[2][3];\n  if (new Set([b0, b1, b2]).size !== 3) continue;\n  const init = { columns: cols, slot: null };\n  // Check how many of the 3 initial column clicks lead to IMMEDIATE 0-move deadlock!\n  let instantDeadCount = 0;\n  let winCol = -1;\n  for (let c = 0; c < 3; c++) {\n    const next = stepState(init, { type: 'col', idx: c }, 3);\n    const nextValid = getValidActions(next, 3);\n    if (nextValid.length === 0) instantDeadCount++;\n    else winCol = c;\n  }\n  // We want 2 of the 3 initial clicks to be INSTANT 0-move deadlocks, and the 3rd click to be WINNABLE!\n  if (instantDeadCount === 2 && winCol !== -1) {\n    const winPath = canWin(init, 3);\n    if (winPath) {\n      console.log('FOUND PERFECT LEVEL:', JSON.stringify(cols));\n      console.log('Win path (' + winPath.length + '):', winPath.join(' -> '));\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Find 3-column 4-color level with instant 0-move deadlocks and a winning path"}}]}
